EXT. RAINBOW BRIDGE - TOLLBOOTH - DAY

A shimmering arc of color across a cloud-sea. At its midpoint, a little wooden booth with a thatched roof and a bell on a string. A hand-painted sign: "TOLL: ONE TRUE STORY."

ELDRIC FENN, 70, tollkeeper, patchwork coat, spectacles pushed up on his forehead, whittles a stick. He hums, content. Gulls wheel. A mild wind.

He pauses. Listens. Hums again.

ELDRIC
Quiet day.

He whittles on. Somewhere below, a faint bell tolls. He blows shavings off the stick.

ELDRIC (CONT'D)
Quiet week, really.

Footsteps on the bridge. A figure rises over the curve of the arch. NUALA BRAE, 30s, wanderer, patched cloak, walking staff, a satchel across her back. She stops at the booth and sets down her staff.

NUALA
Good morning.

ELDRIC
Morning, traveler. Toll's one true story.

NUALA
Any story?

ELDRIC
True ones only. I can tell.

NUALA
How?

ELDRIC
The bell. It rings when you lie.

NUALA
Ah.

She thinks. The wind lifts the edge of her cloak.

NUALA (CONT'D)
When I was nine, I fell into a well and stayed there three days. I told the village a fox pulled me out. It was a fox.

Eldric holds the bell string. Nothing rings.

ELDRIC
Hm.

NUALA
Is that enough?

ELDRIC
It's a decent story.

NUALA
But?

ELDRIC
The fox was a lie, wasn't it?

Nuala says nothing. The bell does not ring.

ELDRIC (CONT'D)
No. The fox was true. It's the nine that was a lie. You were twelve.

NUALA
Fifteen.

The bell rings, softly, one clear note.

ELDRIC
There it is.

NUALA
I'll pay in cheese instead.

ELDRIC
That'll do.

She hands over a wedge wrapped in cloth. He lifts the gate. She takes her staff and crosses. Halfway past him she stops.

NUALA
Do you get many visitors?

ELDRIC
Fewer every year.

NUALA
Why?

ELDRIC
They've run out of true stories.

NUALA
Perhaps they've run out of nerve.

She walks on. Eldric watches her go until she is a small bright thing on the far end of the bridge. Then he picks up his stick and starts whittling again.
